WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
You know the feeling you get
when you learn something new

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.180
about a health problem
you’ve been trying to reverse?

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.660
Maybe high blood pressure,
diabetes, or heart disease.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.320
Well, there’s nothing I like better
than bringing you the information

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.080
that will help you do just that.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.600
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.480
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.020
Today we discover the
mighty power of oatmeal.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.540
Did you know that
before there was insulin,

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.329
there was the ‘oatmeal cure’?

00:00:30.617 --> 00:00:32.580
The life of many diabetics was saved

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.520
or prolonged by
Carl von Noorden’s oatmeal diet,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.920
which he apparently
stumbled upon accidentally.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.740
Some of his diabetic patients
had gastrointestinal issues;

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:41.760
so, he put them on oatmeal,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.280
and instead of the sugars
spilling over into their urine

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.620
getting worse,
they started getting better.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:51.060
He published his findings
in 1903, which was received

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.860
with a great deal of skepticism.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.180
But the skeptics were overcome,
however, in the following years

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.860
by the weight of the evidence.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.320
A turning point
came when a doctor

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.380
as notable as James B. Herrick
gave it a try.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:08.040
Dr. Herrick is acclaimed for his
description of sickle cell anemia,

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.560
which was originally known
as Herrick's syndrome.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.640
When Dr. Herrick began to try out
the oatmeal diet on his patients,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.120
he was very skeptical, but was
astonished by the results.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.900
Intense skepticism
was how Herrick put it.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.000
His first experience
in prescribing it

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.320
was far from encouraging.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.960
After taking one or two meals,
the patient said,

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.820
“Doctor, I will die before I taste
another spoonful of that oatmeal mush.”

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.060
And indeed, tragically, she did.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.360
Other doctors echoed
patient reticence

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.480
to tolerate so monotonous
an equine diet.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:44.460
But in general, Herrick said,

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.080
he went on to experience
little difficulty

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.700
in putting patients
on the oatmeal diet

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.560
and in keeping them
there for a few weeks.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.140
And nothing, he reported,
was more surprising

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.560
or more gratifying than
the salutary effects he witnessed

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.320
of the oatmeal diet
in diabetes of the young,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.640
leading to the 1909 proclamation
that no case of juvenile

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.040
or adolescent diabetes
should be deprived

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.980
of the benefits
of the oatmeal cure.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.240
The great Elliott Joslin,
founder of the oldest

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.220
and largest diabetes clinic
in the world,

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:17.880
described the effects of the oatmeal
as sometimes magical,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.480
describing the oatmeal cure
as an unsolved mystery,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:22.860
referred to back
then as one

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.660
of the greatest puzzles
in diabetes.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:26.880
They did have
some clues though.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.240
They found that animal protein
had to be strictly excluded,

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:34.560
as it annihilates the favorable
action of oatmeal-type diets.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.080
They used to use eggs
with the oatmeal diet,

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.480
but they got better results
without them.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.960
And now we know, over
a century later that indeed,

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.500
animal protein intake
intensiﬁes insulin resistance,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.680
which is the cause of prediabetes
and type 2 diabetes,

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.280
whereas plant-based foods
enhance insulin sensitivity,

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.240
which is the opposite.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.180
Animal-protein intake
is not just associated

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:00.120
with insulin resistance
and a clear association

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.220
with an increased risk
of type 2 diabetes

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:05.580
(and this included animal protein
from meat, dairy, and ﬁsh—

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.480
higher insulin resistance and risk
of prediabetes and type 2 diabetes).

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.320
But not just an association;
you can put it to the test.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.820
Swapping in beans for beef
improves cardiometabolic risk factors.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.020
And it doesn’t take much.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.620
Replace just two servings
of red meat with lentils, chickpeas,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.540
split peas, or beans
a few days a week,

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:28.980
and you can significantly improve
fasting blood sugars and insulin levels,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.080
along with the improvements
you’d expect,

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.200
like lowering of cholesterol
and triglycerides.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.900
Based on over a dozen
randomized controlled trials,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.060
even just swapping
like a third of protein

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.440
from animal to plant sources

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.380
can significantly improve
blood sugar control.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.760
What’s the difference

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.280
between animal protein
and plant protein?

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:50.160
We think it’s the branched-chain
amino acids

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.780
concentrated in animal protein.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.140
How do we know branched-chain
amino acids are playing a role?

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.920
Because if you give vegans
branched-chain amino acid supplements,

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:01.680
you can make them as insulin
resistant as meat eaters.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.520
Their insulin sensitivity dropped
to the level resembling omnivores

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.160
and only improved again
after stopping the supplements.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:09.960
But wait a second.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.920
I thought insulin resistance stems
from excess accumulation of fat

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.680
inside your muscle cells,
particularly saturated fat.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.020
Insulin resistance
directly correlates

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:21.720
with increased saturated fat
inside your muscles.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.880
I’ve got tons of videos on this,

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.100
but basically you can
show a substantial

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.740
and consistent impairment
of insulin action,

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.560
substantial and
consistent insulin resistance

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:32.940
after just a single day

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.040
consuming a diet high
in saturated fat.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.100
In fact, even a single meal
rich in saturated fat

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:39.780
reduces insulin sensitivity.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.480
A single dose of butter,
for example,

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.780
impairs insulin sensitivity
even in healthy subjects.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:48.180
And over time,
reducing cholesterol

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.940
and fat intake may even enhance
the ability of your pancreas

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:52.740
to pump out insulin
in the first place.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.040
Now, the saturated fat
getting lodged in your muscles

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.200
may come from the foods
going into your mouth,

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:00.660
or if you have
excess abdominal fat,

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:04.080
from previous meals spilling
over into your blood stream.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.700
But either way, what does
animal protein have to do with it?

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.420
It turns out a branched-chain
amino acid breakdown product

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.880
appears to stimulate fat uptake

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.920
and accumulation inside
the muscle cells.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:21.480
But oatmeal doesn’t have any
saturated animal fat or animal protein.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.660
Okay, but neither does
any plant food.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.600
Why might oatmeal work
particularly well?

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.960
That’s the question
I explore next.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.580
It is now widely accepted
that diets high in animal fat

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.680
and processed foods are
an important risk factor

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.600
for development
of type 2 diabetes.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.620
And it’s not just animal fat,
but animal protein intake

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.240
intensiﬁes insulin resistance,

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.060
which predisposes
people to type 2 diabetes.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:51.240
No wonder studies have shown
that elevated consumption

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.120
of animal products and low intake
of unprocessed plant foods

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.800
increases the risk of not only
cardiovascular disease but diabetes.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.420
But of all the whole plant foods
to pick, why choose oatmeal

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:06.060
to treat diabetes, which, as I
discussed in my last video,

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.680
was used for the treatment of diabetes
before insulin was discovered.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.160
We’ve long known that higher consumption
of whole grains, including oats,

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.640
is associated with a lower risk
of diabetes, but you don’t know...

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.100
until you put it to the test.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.980
There have been over a dozen
randomized controlled trials

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.420
looking at the metabolic effects

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:27.240
of oats intake in patients
with type 2 diabetes,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.880
and oats were found
to significantly improve

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:33.480
both short-term blood sugar control
and long-term blood sugar control

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:36.120
in addition to lowering
cholesterol levels.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.080
We think the benefits arise
from a fermentable ﬁber in oats

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:43.080
called beta glucan, because you
can get cholesterol-lowering

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.940
even if you just give
the oat fiber straight,

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.220
as well as an improvement
in blood sugar control

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.740
and insulin sensitivity
in both type 2 diabetics,

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.540
as well as type 1 diabetics.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:55.200
How exactly does
the fiber do that?

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:58.500
Well, we know one of the underlying
cholesterol-lowering mechanisms

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:02.700
of oatmeal consumption might be
its microbiome-manipulating ability,

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.860
in other words, having
a beneficial effect

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.480
on our intestinal bacteria.

00:07:06.511 --> 00:07:10.260
The anti-inflammatory effects
of the short-chain fatty acids

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.540
that our good gut flora
make from fiber.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:16.080
There are dozens of randomized
controlled trials

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.060
showing the types
of fiber found in oats

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:21.540
and beans can improve long-term
blood sugar control in diabetics—

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:24.180
in fact, nearly double
the FDA threshold

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:27.540
required for new blood sugar-
lowering drugs. Why?

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:32.100
Because the gut bacteria selectively
promoted by dietary fiber intake

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.040
can help alleviate
type 2 diabetes.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:39.240
In fact, on the basis of 50 distinct
bacterial markers of the feces,

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.960
you can tell who does
and does not have diabetes.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.840
But change your diet,
and you can change

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:48.720
your gut flora within one day.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:52.020
We feed them with fiber,
and in return,

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:54.720
they feed us right back
with these short-chain fatty acids

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.940
like butyrate that have
all these wonderful effects.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:02.160
Put people on a diet packed with oats,
beans, fruits, vegetables, and nuts,

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:03.780
and the number
of fiber-feeders

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:06.780
churning out the beneficial short-
chain fatty acids shoots up,

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:11.580
and fasting diabetic blood sugars
drop about 25 percent within one month.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:14.220
And the more fiber-
feeders they fostered,

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:16.200
the better their
blood sugar control.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.040
When the fiber-promoted
short-chain fatty acid producers

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:22.200
were present in greater
diversity and abundance,

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:26.520
participants had better improvement
in their hemoglobin A1c levels

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:29.880
(which is a measure of
longer-term blood sugar control).

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:34.440
Then, before and after
fecal transplant studies

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.540
helped nail down
cause and effect.

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:40.620
The oat fiber itself has been
shown to act as a prebiotic,

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:42.780
boosting the growth
of beneficial bacteria

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:44.820
like lactobacillus
and bifidobacteria.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:46.980
So, between the lack
of animal protein,

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:48.360
lack of animal fat,

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:51.300
and bursting at the seams
with prebiotic fiber,

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:54.240
it’s no wonder that oatmeal
diets grew to become

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:58.020
part of the clinical routine
in the treatment of diabetics.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:00.060
However, over time,
this practice

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:02.580
has later become
increasingly forgotten,

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:04.860
a disappearance that’s been
compared to the fate

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:07.260
of unpopular theories
in successive editions

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:08.940
of Soviet encyclopedias.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:12.780
Despite advances in therapy,
we still have many people

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:14.460
with poorly controlled diabetes.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:17.640
Thankfully this
forgotten tool is back.

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:22.284
I’ll review all the new
oatmeal diet studies next.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.680
Thousands of years ago in ancient Egypt,
diabetes was described

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:32.460
as a “too great emptying of urine”
or, more poetically,

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:36.840
as being “like the River Nile
between the thighs.”

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:39.720
The recommended remedy,
ironically, was a diet

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:43.500
consisting of wheat grains,
grapes, honey, and berries.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:47.640
The guy who coined the term
“diabetes” about 500 years later

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:50.460
also prescribed
a high-carbohydrate diet.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:53.040
Then, right up until
we had insulin,

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:56.400
doctors were saving the lives
of diabetics with an oatmeal diet.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:02.340
This wouldn’t make any sense
until Sir Harold Himsworth arrived

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:05.700
on the scene, the first
to separate out type 1 diabetes

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:10.800
from type 2 diabetes and define
this concept of insulin resistance.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.320
After just a few days
on a high-fat diet,

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:15.660
you can get twice the blood
sugar spike in response

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:16.560
to drinking sugar water,

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.140
compared to after eating
a high-carb diet.

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:24.480
Now that type 2 diabetes is like
the Black Death of the 21st century

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:26.460
in terms of devastating
health impacts,

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:29.520
what about revisiting
the almost forgotten,

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:32.580
short-term dietary oatmeal
intervention as an economical,

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:36.720
yet—spoiler alert—
highly effective tool to achieve

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:40.380
better blood sugar control
in patients with type 2 diabetes?

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:42.900
Basically, patients
are offered up to about

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:45.300
two and a half cups of oatmeal
three times a day

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:48.000
as their meals
with nothing but some herbs

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:50.700
and maybe small amounts of raw
vegetables just to mix things up.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.040
For how long?
Just a couple of days.

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:56.040
Note that’s only
like a thousand calories;

00:10:56.040 --> 00:11:00.300
so, the result is a hypocaloric,
plant-based dietary intervention

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:03.120
that is low in fat—in fact,
no added fat—no salt,

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.340
and excludes animal protein.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:08.100
Is a few days
of oatmeal really going

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.120
to make much
of a difference?

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:13.020
Check out this case report
of an oatmeal intervention

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:15.780
for severe insulin
resistance in the ICU.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:18.900
Within 48 hours of admission,
the patient developed

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:21.120
such severe insulin
resistance she required

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.400
more than 200 units
of insulin per day.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:27.420
Up until then, the patient received
standard diabetic tube feeds,

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:29.820
which obviously
were not working.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:34.800
So instead, they dropped oatmeal
and vegetables down the tube instead,

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:36.360
presumably using
a really good blender.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:39.360
And lo and behold, it worked.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:39.900
But you've got
to see the numbers.

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:42.840
Yeah, her first blood sugars
of the day dropped

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:46.200
from up around 250 down to about
100 five days later.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:52.500
But that near-normal blood sugar
was on 160 fewer units of insulin,

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:54.360
down from over 200 units a day.

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:59.160
Lower blood sugars
on 160 fewer units of insulin!

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:03.360
Okay, I can see how if you’re
trying to save a life in the ICU,

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:05.940
an oatmeal diet
can be near-miraculous.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:08.340
But just in regular diabetics,

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:10.740
what good is eating
oatmeal for a few days

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:12.900
if you just go back
to your regular diet?

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:16.440
Several studies have suggested
that the beneficial effects

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:19.620
could last like a month
after the few days of oatmeal.

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:22.500
For example, in this randomized
controlled crossover trial

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:26.160
not only did insulin needs drop
about 40 percent in just two days

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.320
compared to just
restricting calories alone

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:30.291
with a hypocaloric
diabetic diet,

00:12:30.291 --> 00:12:32.700
but also a measure
long-term blood sugar control

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:35.280
taken four weeks later
reflected the benefit.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.920
So, we’re talking a highly
significant reduction

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:39.900
of required daily
insulin doses

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:42.360
with beneficial effects
shown weeks later.

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:46.140
Who cares if you have to take
huge doses of insulin though?

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:48.840
Because insulin causes weight gain,

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:52.680
which just makes the underlying
insulin resistance worse.

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:54.120
So, it’s like this vicious cycle.

00:12:54.120 --> 00:12:57.600
But instead, with the oatmeal
you’re actually treating the cause,

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:01.020
not to mention the incidence
of cancer and overall mortality

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:03.480
associated with having
such high levels of insulin

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:04.500
in your body all the time.

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:07.680
Other new studies have
shown the same thing.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.740
Two days of oatmeal significantly
reducing the required amount

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:12.840
of insulin and
improved blood sugar levels

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:16.140
with beneficial effects
noted for up to four weeks.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:17.040
For example, here.

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:19.800
Patients with uncontrolled
type 2 diabetes,

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:21.540
with the two-day
oatmeal diet

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:23.940
leading to a 40 percent
reduction of insulin dose,

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:27.360
accompanied with almost
normalization of average blood sugars.

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:30.360
Although the intervention
only lasted for two days,

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:34.380
they observed a lasting significant
reduction of insulin dosage

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:37.500
and ameliorated mean
blood sugars for weeks

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:39.240
after they were dismissed
from the study.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:42.885
And this was after they resumed
their regular diets.

00:13:42.885 --> 00:13:45.060
Look at this.
A massive drop in insulin needs

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.460
after the oatmeal for two days,
but look, a month later

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:50.640
they were still needing
like 40 percent less insulin.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:54.120
Wait a second.
How could this short intervention lead

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:57.240
to such dramatic results
that somehow continue on for weeks?

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:00.060
Although short-term dietary
oatmeal interventions

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:03.000
cannot be compared to whole food,
plant-based diets

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.640
in terms of maximizing
the intake of protective foods—

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:07.860
I mean that’s ideally
what people should try to eat

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.440
to reverse their
type 2 diabetes completely—

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:14.460
but they both strictly
exclude the animal-based foods

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:17.100
that seem to increase the risk
of developing diabetes.

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:20.160
So, even cutting out
saturated fat for like two days

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:23.640
may so reduce insulin
resistance you can free ride

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:27.660
on that for at least a few weeks,
even if you go back to eating crap.

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:31.920
WARNING, though.
If you try this oatmeal diet,

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:35.880
your physician has to be ready
to rapidly deprescribe

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:39.060
your blood sugar drugs else
you become dangerously overmedicated.

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:42.960
Imagine if this woman was still
getting 200 units of insulin.

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:45.840
Her sugars would crash
so low she’d be dead.

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:49.560
So, oatmeal interventions
should not be performed

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:51.720
in patients that might
have difficulties

00:14:51.720 --> 00:14:53.880
in reporting symptoms
of low blood sugars,

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:55.860
who you can’t
closely monitor.

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:58.260
So, the downside
of trying oatmeal days

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:01.140
is that it may
work a little too well;

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:06.000
so, it must be done under close
medical supervision.

